<section class="chapter" id="overview">
  <div class="panel panel--cool">
    <div class="chapter__inner">
      <h2 class="section-title">1. Overview &amp; Outcomes</h2>

      <h3>1.1 Why a Candidate Journey Playbook?</h3>
      <p>
        Most companies have a rough idea of their “candidate journey,” but few have a single shared view of what candidates should experience,
        what messages they receive (and when), which steps are automated vs human-led, and who owns each touchpoint.
      </p>

      <h3>1.2 Outcomes we’re aiming for</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Predictable, transparent experience from first touch through onboarding.</li>
        <li>Fewer “black box” moments where candidates don’t know what’s happening.</li>
        <li>Clear automation opportunities that save time without sacrificing warmth.</li>
        <li>Reusable templates and workflows instead of one-off email threads.</li>
        <li>Cleaner measurement inputs for diagnostics (time-in-stage, drop-off, comms coverage).</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="chapter" id="stages">
  <div class="panel panel--cool">
    <div class="chapter__inner">
      <h2 class="section-title">2. Journey Stages</h2>
      <p>
        The journey is broken into six core stages. Some candidates skip stages (internal moves) or loop (multiple panels),
        but the model stays consistent.
      </p>

      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>#</th>
              <th>Stage</th>
              <th>Primary Goal</th>
              <th>Typical Owner</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>0</td>
              <td>Awareness &amp; Interest</td>
              <td>Make the company and role feel relevant and worth exploring.</td>
